<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      CAMERA is the only permission this application ships with.

      There is deliberately no INTERNET permission. Chapter 6's deployment
      view argues that the privacy claim in 1.2 is enforced by the platform
      rather than asserted in prose: an application without the permission
      cannot open a socket, whatever its code says, and that is checkable by
      anyone with the APK in under a minute. Adding INTERNET here would
      silently withdraw that argument, so it is not a line to add casually
      when a future feature wants a network call.

      4.4 chose a pre-authored cue bank partly to keep this true. Nothing
      in the live path needs a network, and the bank ships in the APK.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" />

    <!--
      Not declaring INTERNET is not the same as not having it, and on
      2026-11-04 the first build on hardware had it. The merged manifest
      carried INTERNET and ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, both granted, neither
      written here.

      They arrive with com.google.mediapipe:tasks-vision, through
      tasks-core, which depends on com.google.android.datatransport. That is
      Google's Cloud Client Telemetry transport: a library whose purpose is
      to batch data and upload it. It brought a backend-discovery service, a
      JobScheduler service and an alarm receiver with it.

      Removing them here is what makes the claim in 1.2 true of the artefact
      rather than of this file. The manifest merger is the only place it can
      be done, because the permission is not ours to leave out.

      The library is left on the classpath rather than excluded from the
      build. Denying it the permission and the components is a platform
      guarantee; removing classes that tasks-core may construct is a crash
      waiting for the first session. If MediaPipe ever needs the network to
      do inference, that is a finding about MediaPipe and belongs in the
      report, not a reason to restore the line quietly.

      Check after any dependency change, and do not trust this file alone:
        app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/*/AndroidManifest.xml
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      These three are not requested by any dependency. The merger implies
      them, and its own report says why:

        reason: org.tensorflow.lite.gpu.api has a targetSdkVersion < 4

      A transitive AAR ships a manifest with no modern targetSdkVersion,
      which trips the compatibility rule that granted pre-Donut applications
      storage and phone-state access implicitly. Nothing in this application
      or in that library calls an API they protect, so removing them cannot
      raise a SecurityException.

      They are removed rather than tolerated because an APK that asks to read
      phone state is a question the privacy requirements in 3.4.4 should not
      have to answer, and "a dependency added it" is a poor answer to it.
      Session records live in application-specific external storage, which
      has needed no permission since API 19 and this application's minimum is
      26, so the two storage entries buy nothing either.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="FORMA"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Forma">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Forma">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The upload machinery that came with the telemetry transport. With
          no INTERNET permission these could not reach anything, but a
          scheduler that wakes the device to retry an upload it can never
          complete is still work this application did not ask for.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.backends.TransportBackendDiscovery"
            tools:node="remove" />
        <service
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.scheduling.jobscheduling.JobInfoSchedulerService"
            tools:node="remove" />
        <receiver
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.scheduling.jobscheduling.AlarmManagerSchedulerBroadcastReceiver"
            tools:node="remove" />
    </application>
</manifest>
